{
  "payloads": [
    {
      "text": "Here is an excerpt from Part One of \"Babel, or the Necessity of Violence\" by R.F. Kuang, which includes the introduction of Robin's relationship with language:\n\n---\n\nA man arrived in the afternoon — an Englishman, tall and thin and gleaming with sweat, attended by two Cantonese clerks. He wore a coat of dark grey wool, unsuited to the city's damp heat, and a pair of spectacles whose lenses caught the light. He stood in the doorway a long time before he spoke, considering the boy with the detached interest of a man examining a silver bar at the assay office.\n\n'You speak English,' the Englishman said. It was not a question. It was a fact that had been reported to him, presumably by one of the neighbours in the lane, and he was confirming it.\n\nThe boy did speak English. His mother had taught him as a child, using a worn grammar book she kept wrapped in oilcloth. He had had no reason to speak it in months, and the word he managed in reply felt strange in his mouth: 'Yes.'\n\n'And Mandarin?'\n\n'Yes.'\n\n'And your Cantonese is native.'\n\nThe boy nodded.\n\n---\n\nThis passage sets the stage for Robin's complex relationship with language and translation from the very beginning. The emphasis on his multilingual abilities and the way they are framed within the context of the Englishman's detached observation foreshadows the novel's exploration of language as a tool of power and colonialism. This thematic introduction aligns closely with Kuang's anti-colonial thesis, mirroring Robin's journey throughout the book as he navigates his identity and the implications of his linguistic skills.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
